{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Who is like You among the mighty, YHWH, who is like You, adorned in holiness.</b> The added heh in both of them alludes to the attribute of Judgment. The heh of the first 'who is like You' is a weak attribute of Judgment, and therefore it is with a rafeh, receiving from the attribute of Judgment above, which is a harsh attribute; and therefore the second 'who is like You' is with a dagesh, and it receives from the qodesh, and therefore it says 'adorned in holiness.' And understand this." "<b>Awesome in praises.</b> All creatures are afraid to declare His praises, lest they diminish the One they praise. And so David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 65:2): ״לך דומיה תהלה אלהים בציון״ — 'To You silence is praise, Elohim, in Zion.' The praise that a person can offer to HaShem, may He be exalted, is silence, because praise falls into three categories: the first category is excessive, the second category is fitting, the third is deficient. Excessive is when the praiser praises the praised one to a rank greater than his rank; fitting is that one not exceed his rank; deficient is that one lower him from his rank and not fulfill his measure in the rank of his praise. The first two categories constitute falsehood in the descriptions of HaShem, may He be exalted, and there remains only the third category, which is the deficient, for the one who praises Him falls short in his praise and does not describe Him as befits Him according to His measure — and therefore it says 'to You silence is praise.' And it is written (Psalms 106:2): ״מי ימלל גבורות ה׳״ — 'Who can utter the mighty acts of YHWH' — and it is written (Nehemiah 9:5): ״ומרומם על כל ברכה ותהלה״ — 'and exalted above all blessing and praise.' And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'to You silence is praise' — the antidote for everything is silence; as for a flawless gem that has no blemish in it, however much you praise it, you impair it." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> may his memory protect us, explained 'awesome in praises' — because the tyrants of the earth are terrifying through oppression and perversion, it says that HaShem, may He be exalted, is awesome in the things for which He is praised — end of his words, of blessed memory. And what it says, 'doing a wonder (עשה פלא)' — this was a great wonder, for the wind is one and with it He did a thing and its opposite: for the wind that froze the waters, as it says, ״וברוח אפיך נערמו מים״ and ״קפאו תהומות״ — 'and with the breath of Your nostrils the waters were heaped up; the depths froze' — is the same one that thawed them and made them flow, as it says, ״נשפת ברוחך כסמו ים״ — 'You blew with Your wind, the sea covered them' — and behold, this is one thing and its opposite." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say that what it says, 'doing a wonder (עשה פלא),' and it did not say 'doing wonders (עשה נפלאות)' or 'doing great things (עשה גדולות),' the intent of this is regarding the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, which was one wonder encompassing many wonders. And you already know that the well-known miracles are called נפלאות (wonders) because they are revealed things coming from a hidden power, for the term פלא (wonder) is a hidden and concealed matter, and all the miracles and wonders indeed come from the power of the hidden, which is the alef, and it encompasses פלא (wonder), and because wisdom is drawn from the alef, therefore Scripture says (Job 33): ״ואאלפך חכמה״ — 'and I will teach you wisdom.'" "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'Awesome in praises' — Rabbi Yudan said: Awesome are You over all Your praises. How so? A king of flesh and blood — they praise him that he is mighty, yet he is weak; that he is rich, yet he is poor; that he is merciful, yet he is cruel. But the Holy One, blessed be He, is not so: the more a person praises Him, the more there is in Him yet further beyond — therefore He is 'awesome over all His praises.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, 